```typescript
type Id = string | number;           // union — an Id is EITHER a string OR a number
type Named = { name: string };
type Aged = { age: number };
type Person = Named & Aged;          // intersection — a Person has BOTH properties

function printId(id: Id) {
  if (typeof id === "string") return id.toUpperCase(); // narrowed to string here
  return id.toFixed(2);                                   // narrowed to number here
}
```

**Union (`|`)** models "could be any of these shapes" — you can only safely call a method that exists on **every** member of the union until you narrow it down (next topic covers narrowing in depth). **Intersection (`&`)** models "has all of these shapes at once" — `Person` must satisfy `Named` and `Aged` simultaneously, so it needs both `name` and `age`. Unions model variation; intersections model composition.

A generic lets a function or type stay reusable across many concrete types **without losing type information** — the alternative, `any`, is reusable too, but throws away every guarantee.

```typescript
function firstElement<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined {
  return arr[0];
}

const n = firstElement([1, 2, 3]);       // T inferred as number, n: number | undefined
const s = firstElement(["a", "b"]);      // T inferred as string, s: string | undefined
```

`T` is a **type parameter** — a placeholder filled in per call site, inferred here from the argument's actual element type. Compare this to `function firstElement(arr: any[]): any`, which compiles and runs identically but tells the caller nothing: `n.toFixed()` would type-check even though `n` might be a string, because `any` opts every value touching it out of checking entirely. Generics keep the connection between input type and output type that `any` severs.

```typescript
function describe(value: string | number | null) {
  if (value === null) return "nothing";
  if (typeof value === "string") return value.toUpperCase();
  return value.toFixed(2);
}
```

At each `if`, TypeScript **narrows** the type of `value` for the rest of that branch, based on the check performed — a `typeof`/`===`/`instanceof` check inside a condition doesn't just affect runtime behavior, it changes what the type checker believes `value` can be for every line reachable only through that branch.

